  <data name="btnLocalizableControl.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>DynamicStringLocalization = Disabled (or Custom)</value>
  </data>
  <data name="btnLocalizableControl.ToolTipText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When BaseUserControl.DynamicStringLocalization is Disabled or Custom, it can inherit the Local/Assembly scope of a parent user control or form.

In this case, the form has a LocalScope localization setting, whereas the user control has no scoped dynamic string localization, so its texts are taken from the form's resource sets, if present.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="btnLocalizableControl.Description" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This user control uses no scoped localization, so it inherits the LocalScope settings of the parent form, using form-level resources.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="lblInstruction.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>DynamicStringLocalization is set to LocalScope on this form, meaning, every localizable text element is applied dynamically from a form-specific resource set, including this text. If you see a [T] prefix before the text, your Windows locale uses a non-English UI culture, and an untranslated resource set has just been generated for that language.

You see 3 user controls below, all have different DynamicStringLocalization settings. Feel free to change them by clicking their blank border area and setting the DynamicStringLocalization property.

Also, feel free to edit the resources by the Edit Resources button. You can override even the compiled English resources, and apply the changes at runtime.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="lblLocalizableControlCaption.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>This is the Form-level string resource for lblLocalizableControlCaption.Text</value>
  </data>
  <data name="lblLanguage.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Language:</value>
  </data>
  <data name="cmbLanguage.ToolTipText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>If you select a language that has no localization yet, the new resources are automatically generated in the Resources folder of the built application as .resx files.

Untranslated entries are copied from the invariant resource set and get a [T] prefix.

The new resource sets are saved on language change or when you exit the application, and can be saved also on demand.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="LocalizationExample.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Localization Example</value>
  </data>
  <data name="chbCustom.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Custom Override</value>
  </data>
  <data name="chbFilter.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Existing Only</value>
  </data>
  <data name="chbFilter.ToolTipText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>When checked, the Languages selector shows the languages with existing localizations only.
Uncheck to allow generating localization for a new language.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="chbCustom.ToolTipText" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Check to subscribe LocalizationHelper.LocalizationRequested, and provide a custom localization programmatically.

This example implementation just returns some information of the localization context.

Note that enabling this option provides texts for all localizable string properties of the form.
Disabling this option will not clear the additional texts that have no resource entries (e.g. tool tip texts).</value>
  </data>
